![Collections icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/action-bookmark-empty.svg)\n-   ![Cite icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/format_quote.svg)\n-   ![Show article permalink icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/share.svg)\n    \n    ## PERMALINK\n    \n     ![Copy icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/content_copy.svg) Copy\n    \n\n![Open article navigation icon](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/pd-medc-pmc-cloudpmc-viewer/production/9b1d9d87/var/data/static/img/usa-icons/list.svg)\n\nAs a library, NLM provides access to scientific literature. Inclusion in an NLM database does not imply endorsement of, or agreement with, the contents by NLM or the National Institutes of Health.  \nLearn more: [PMC Disclaimer](/about/disclaimer/) | [PMC Copyright Notice](/about/copyright/)\n\n![Molecular Therapy. Methods & Clinical Development logo](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/banners/logo-mtm.gif)\n\nMol Ther Methods Clin Dev\n\n. 2025 Nov 10;33(4):101629. doi: [10.1016/j.omtm.2025.101629](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.omtm.2025.101629)\n\n-   [Search in PMC](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/search/?term=\"Mol%20Ther%20Methods%20Clin%20Dev\"[jour])\n-   [Search in PubMed](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Mol%20Ther%20Methods%20Clin%20Dev\"[jour])\n-   [View in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=\"Mol%20Ther%20Methods%20Clin%20Dev\"[Title%20Abbreviation])\n-   [Add to search](?term=\"Mol%20Ther%20Methods%20Clin%20Dev\"[jour])\n\n# The amazing AAV capsids: Into the structure-verse\n\n[Jane Hsi-Bell](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Hsi-Bell%20J\"[Author])\n\n### Jane Hsi-Bell\n\n1Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA\n\nFind articles by [Jane Hsi-Bell](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Hsi-Bell%20J\"[Author])\n\n1,2, [Mario Mietzsch](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Mietzsch%20M\"[Author])\n\n### Mario Mietzsch\n\n1Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA\n\nFind articles by [Mario Mietzsch](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"Mietzsch%20M\"[Author])\n\n1,2,∗, [Robert McKenna](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"McKenna%20R\"[Author])\n\n### Robert McKenna\n\n1Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA\n\nFind articles by [Robert McKenna](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=\"McKenna%20R\"[Author])\n\n1,∗∗\n\n-   Author information\n-   Article notes\n-   Copyright and License information\n\n1Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA\n\n∗\n\nCorresponding author: Mario Mietzsch, Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA. mario.mietzsch@ufl.edu\n\n∗∗\n\nCorresponding author: Robert McKenna, Department of Biochemistry and Molecular Biology, College of Medicine, Center for Structural Biology, McKnight Brain Institute, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL 32610, USA. rmckenna@ufl.edu\n\n2\n\nThese authors contributed equally\n\nCollection date 2025 Dec 11.\n\n© 2025 The Author(s)\n\nThis is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).\n\n[PMC Copyright notice](/about/copyright/)\n\nPMCID: PMC12682079  PMID: [41362290](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41362290/)\n\n## Abstract\n\nOver the past ∼25 years, structural studies of adeno-associated virus (AAV) capsids have greatly aided our understanding of the biology of these single-stranded DNA viruses and provided insights into their utilization as gene therapy vectors for the treatment of various human diseases. Recent advances in cryo-electron microscopy have yielded a library of currently ∼150 high-resolution AAV capsid structures, with more than 50% determined in the last 5 years alone. Comparative analyses of capsids from primate and nonprimate origins have revealed both conserved architectural elements, such as the canonical jelly-roll fold, and critical surface variations that affect receptor interaction, antibody recognition, and intracellular trafficking. These structures, as well as those in complex with glycan and proteinaceous receptors, purification agents, and antibodies, have been instrumental in rational capsid engineering, guiding the design of new variants with enhanced transduction efficiency, tissue specificity, and reduced detection by pre-existing neutralizing antibodies. This review summarizes the available AAV capsid structures to date, highlighting landmark discoveries over the years, and offers perspectives on how structural biology will continue to drive innovation in AAV gene therapy.\n\n**Keywords:** adeno-associated virus, AAV vector, gene therapy, capsid, receptor, pre-existing antibodies, capsid engineering, cryo-EM, X-ray crystallography\n\n## Graphical abstract\n\n![graphic file with name fx1.jpg](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/28f4e157dfe5/fx1.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/undfig1/)\n\n---\n\nMietzsch and colleagues review approximately 25 years of structural studies on AAV capsids. They describe the current library of around 150 high-resolution structures, emphasizing how these structural insights have advanced our understanding of AAV biology and guided capsid engineering efforts to enhance the efficacy of AAV vectors for gene therapy applications.\n\n## Introduction\n\nFirst discovered as a contaminant of laboratory adenovirus preparations in the 1960s,[1](#bib1) adeno-associated viruses (AAVs) are now considered the most promising vectors for *in vivo* gene delivery to treat a wide range of monogenic diseases.[2](#bib2),[3](#bib3) To date, eight AAV-based therapeutics have been approved for commercialization by the European Medicines Agency and/or the Food and Drug Administration: Glybera, an AAV serotype 1 (AAV1) vector for the treatment of lipoprotein lipase deficiency[4](#bib4); Luxturna, an AAV2 vector for the treatment of Leber’s congenital amaurosis[5](#bib5); Zolgensma, an AAV9 vector for the treatment of spinal muscular atrophy type 1[6](#bib6); Upstaza/Kebilidi, an AAV2 vector for the treatment of aromatic L-amino acid (aa) decarboxylase deficiency[7](#bib7); Roctavian, an AAV5 vector for the treatment of hemophilia A[8](#bib8); Hemgenix, an AAV5 vector for the treatment of hemophilia B[9](#bib9); Elevidys, an AAVrh.74 vector for the treatment of Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD)[10](#bib10); and Beqvez, an AAVrh.74 variant vector for the treatment of hemophilia B.[11](#bib11) In addition to these commercially approved AAV gene therapy treatments, the number of clinical trials of AAV-based gene therapies has skyrocketed in recent years,[2](#bib2) exceeding 300 clinical trials as of 2025.[12](#bib12)\n\nAAV vectors are derived from viruses of the *Parvoviridae* family, genus *Dependoparvovirus*, which package a ∼4.7 kb linear, single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) genome inside their nonenveloped icosahedral capsids with *T* = 1 symmetry and a diameter of ∼25 nm.[13](#bib13),[14](#bib14) The AAV genomes ([Figure 1](#fig1)) contain two large open reading frames, the *rep* and *cap* genes, which are flanked by two identical inverted terminal repeats (ITRs).[15](#bib15) The *rep* gene encodes up to four Rep proteins (Rep40, Rep52, Rep68, and Rep78) that are important for genome replication and packaging, while the *cap* gene encodes the capsid proteins.[16](#bib16) These include the viral proteins (VPs) VP1, VP2, and VP3, which are expressed at a ratio of approximately 1:1:10.[17](#bib17),[18](#bib18),[19](#bib19) The three VPs overlap at their C-termini, with VP1 (∼82 kDa) containing the sequences of VP2 (∼67 kDa) and VP3 (∼60 kDa).[14](#bib14) The unique N terminus of VP1 is referred to as the VP1 unique (VP1u) region.[20](#bib20) The N-terminal sequence of VP2 shared between VP1 and VP2 is termed the VP1/2 common region.[15](#bib15) The VP1u contains an essential phospholipase A2 (PLA2) domain,[21](#bib21) and the VP1/2 common region contains nuclear localization signals,[22](#bib22),[23](#bib23) both of which are critical in the AAV viral life cycle.[24](#bib24) While VP1 is essential for AAV infectivity, VP1 and VP2 are not required for capsid assembly.[25](#bib25),[26](#bib26) In addition, the AAV genome ([Figure 1](#fig1)) encodes two smaller proteins: the assembly-activating protein (AAP) and the membrane-associated accessory protein (MAAP).[27](#bib27),[28](#bib28)\n\n### Figure 1.\n\n[![Figure 1](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/3ee14e7c9e8e/gr1.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr1.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig1/)\n\nThe AAV genome and its proteins\n\nAn overview of the AAV genome is shown, with its two open reading frames flanked by inverted terminal repeats (ITRs), promoters, and polyadenylation site (pA). Below, the expressed Rep proteins are depicted in green, the VPs in yellow, and the smaller membrane-associated accessory protein (MAAP) and assembly-activating protein (AAP) in blue. The VP1 unique (VP1u), the VP1/2-common region (VP1/2), and the structurally ordered segment of the capsid proteins are indicated.\n\nFor the generation of AAV vectors, the *rep* and *cap* genes of the wild-type genome are replaced with a transgene vector cassette, and only the flanking ITRs (usually from AAV2) are retained, which act as packaging signals for the recombinant vector genome.[29](#bib29) The Rep proteins (also usually from AAV2) and the VPs (any capsid gene) are expressed in trans.[30](#bib30) Using this setup, the vector genome is packaged into the capsids instead of the wild-type genome.[15](#bib15) Common production pipelines utilize either HEK293 cells or insect Sf9 cells for AAV vector manufacturing.[31](#bib31),[32](#bib32) The selection of the “appropriate” capsid gene is crucial for the resulting AAV vectors, as the generated capsids determine the tropism and antigenicity of the vectors[33](#bib33),[34](#bib34) and can impact the success of a given therapy.\n\nTherefore, substantial research has been conducted on both the characterization and improvement of AAV capsids.[35](#bib35) Determining AAV capsid structures is critical to achieving these goals and is the focus of this review. While structures have also been determined for the nonstructural Rep proteins of the AAVs,[36](#bib36),[37](#bib37) these will not be covered in this review. To date, 155 AAV capsid structures at a resolution ≤ 4 Å have been determined since 2002 ([Figure 2](#fig2)). Until 2016, structural characterizations of AAV capsids were performed using X-ray crystallography. Since then, all AAV capsid structures have been determined using cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), largely due to groundbreaking advancements in direct electron detectors and computational processing. Furthermore, AAVs are excellent specimens for cryo-EM studies because of their relatively small size for a virus, icosahedral symmetry, and ability to stably assemble into homogenous particles.[38](#bib38) Currently at 1.56 Å, the structure of AAV-DJ represents the highest-resolution capsid structure, though this may soon be surpassed.[39](#bib39),[40](#bib40) The rapid increase in available AAV capsid structures over the last ∼5 years underscores the need for a comprehensive review to identify current and emerging trends in structural AAV biology.\n\n### Figure 2.\n\n[![Figure 2](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/6b50df2ed267/gr2.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr2.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig2/)\n\nOverview of determined AAV capsid structures since 2002\n\nA timeline of the cumulative number of structures with a resolution of <4 Å is shown over the years. Important milestones in AAV structural biology are highlighted in outlined black boxes. Structures determined by X-ray crystallography are indicated in gray/black, and structures determined by cryo-EM are shown in green. The total numbers of X-ray and cryo-EM structures are provided on the right.\n\n## The AAV capsid morphology\n\nEarly transmission electron microcopy images of AAVs revealed the nonenveloped nature of the viral particles.[41](#bib41) Following the determination of the first capsid structures of various RNA viruses,[42](#bib42),[43](#bib43) icosahedral symmetry was also assumed for AAV capsids.[44](#bib44) The first structural insights into the AAV capsid were obtained from a low-resolution cryo-EM reconstruction of AAV2 at ∼10 Å resolution, which revealed large protrusions surrounding the three-fold symmetry axes and narrow channels at the five-fold axes.[45](#bib45) In 2002 the structure of the AAV2 capsid was determined by X-ray crystallography at 3 Å resolution,[46](#bib46) marking the beginning of structural characterization of AAV capsids. The AAV2 structure, as well as those of other AAV capsids determined thereafter (e.g., AAV5, AAVrh.8, and AAVhu.37) ([Figure 3](#fig3)A), regardless of the method used, showed that only the shared VP3 region (the ∼520 C-terminal aa) is structurally ordered, while the VP1u, the VP1/2 common region, and approximately 15 aa of the VP3 N terminus are not observed.[14](#bib14) This was further confirmed when AAV1 and AAV3 capsids composed solely of VP3 were compared with capsids containing all three VPs. In these cases, the capsids were structurally indistinguishable from one another.[26](#bib26),[47](#bib47) The VP structure of AAVs, similar to that of all icosahedral ssDNA viruses, contains a jelly-roll motif formed by an eight-stranded (βBIDG and βCHEF) antiparallel β-barrel motif[48](#bib48) ([Figure 3](#fig3)B). In addition, the βBIDG sheet is extended by the βA strand, which is situated antiparallel to the βB strand. The β(A)BIDG sheet forms the interior surface of the capsid and is larger than the βCHEF sheet, which is mostly buried inside the capsid. Furthermore, a conserved alpha-helix (αA) is inserted between the βC and βD strands. These secondary elements form the building block of the capsid and are conserved among AAVs and other genera within the *Parvovirinae* subfamily, including *Amdo-*, *Ave-*, *Boca-*, *Erythro*\\-, *Proto*\\-, and *Tetraparvovirus*, for which structures have been determined.[49](#bib49),[50](#bib50),[51](#bib51),[52](#bib52),[53](#bib53) The surface of the capsid is formed by interconnecting loops between the β-strands. These loops are labeled based on their flanking β-strands—for example, the loop connecting the βE and βF strands is referred to as the EF loop ([Figure 4](#fig4)). In AAVs, the BC, DE, EF, GH, HI, and C-terminal loops contribute to the surface topological features of the capsid.[48](#bib48) The long GH loop, consisting of more than 200 aa, forms several subloops. These surface loops exhibit high aa sequence diversity and structural variability among different AAVs (see [the variable regions of the AAVs](#sec4) section) and among members of the different *Parvovirinae* genera.[49](#bib49),[53](#bib53)\n\n### Figure 3.\n\n[![Figure 3](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/d052d78f4bc3/gr3.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr3.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig3/)\n\nCapsid structures of primate AAVs\n\n(A) Exemplary capsid structures of AAV2 (PDB: [8FYW](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/8FYW)), AAV5 (PDB: [7KP3](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/7KP3)), AAVrh.8 (PDB: [4RSO](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/4RSO)), and AAVhu.37 (PDB: [6U95](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/6U95)) are shown as surface representations generated from their respective PDB files. The capsids are colored radially (blue to red) based on the distance from the center of the capsid, according to the scale bar on the left. The icosahedral 2-fold, 3-fold, and 5-fold symmetry axes are shown on the AAV2 capsid. The images were generated in ChimeraX. (B) A structural superposition of 18 primate-based AAVs, colored as indicated and depicted as ribbon diagrams, is shown. The positions of the β-strands, N termini, C termini, VRs, and icosahedral 2-fold, 3-fold, and 5-fold symmetry axes are labeled. This figure was generated in PyMOL.\n\n### Figure 4.\n\n[![Figure 4](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/7554795bc452/gr4.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr4.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig4/)\n\nThe AAV VP structure\n\nA side view of the AAV VP structure is shown as a ribbon/coil diagram, with the β-strands in shades of dark gray and the α-helixA in red. The individual surface loops are colored, and the positions of the icosahedral symmetry axes and the N-termini and C-termini are indicated.\n\nAlthough the sequences of the VPs from various AAVs differ by up to 50% in aa identity, their capsids share common morphological features.[38](#bib38) To form the icosahedral AAV capsid, 60 VP monomers assemble through 2-, 3-, and 5-fold symmetry interactions.[54](#bib54) These interactions result in the formation of cylindrical channels by five DE loops at the icosahedral 5-fold symmetry axes. The 5-fold channels connect the interior of the capsid to the external environment and are believed to play important roles in genome packaging/ejection, as well as VP1u externalization.[20](#bib20),[55](#bib55),[56](#bib56) Surrounding the 3-fold symmetry axes, the AAV capsids possess large protrusions formed primarily by the GH loops.[57](#bib57) The shallowest regions of the capsid are located at the 2-fold symmetry axes and around the 5-fold pore, to which the C-terminal loop and HI loop contribute, respectively.[58](#bib58) The raised region between these depressed areas is referred to as the 2/5-fold wall, formed by the BC, EF, and partially the C-terminal loops. While all AAVs conserve these overall surface features, different loop conformations and aa sequences lead to alternative appearances of the capsid surface and subsequently result in variable receptor usage, which contribute to distinct host and tissue tropisms as well as antigenic profiles.\n\n## The capsids of primate-based AAVs\n\nThe first AAVs isolated from human or nonhuman primate cells and/or tissues were referred to as AAV serotypes. Historically, AAV serotypes were distinguished by serological studies, physiochemical characterizations of their genomes, restriction digestion patterns, and later by their sequences.[59](#bib59),[60](#bib60),[61](#bib61),[62](#bib62) However, following the identification of AAV6, which differs by only six aa from AAV1 in the VP1 capsid sequence,[62](#bib62) an increasing number of AAV isolates were found—due to advancements in sequencing technologies—that also varied by only a few aa from previously described AAV serotypes. Thus, the designation of new AAV serotypes was discontinued after serotype 13, and the AAVs were instead organized into clades. The clade organization of the AAVs was first proposed in 2004, based on more than 100 unique isolates from humans and nonhuman primates (including rhesus monkeys, cynomolgus and pigtailed macaques, baboons, and chimpanzees).[63](#bib63) They are defined based on a neighbor-joining phylogenetic tree of their capsid protein sequences, as the *rep* gene sequences were often not sequenced or deposited. This resulted in the formation of six clades, with the AAV serotypes as prototypic members (clade A: AAV1 and AAV6; clade B: AAV2; clade C: AAV3 and AAV13; clade D: AAV7; clade E: AAV8 and AAV10; clade F: AAV9).[63](#bib63) AAV4 was classified as a clone and later suggested to form clade G together with AAV11 and AAV12.[38](#bib38) Similarly, AAV5 was classified as a clone, which in the AAV community is sometimes assigned to clade H along with nonprimate AAVs such as AAVgo.1.[64](#bib64)\n\nAs mentioned above, AAV2 was the first AAV serotype whose capsid was structurally characterized.[46](#bib46) Since then, all thirteen AAV serotype capsid structures have been determined except for AAV10.[38](#bib38),[57](#bib57),[65](#bib65),[66](#bib66),[67](#bib67),[68](#bib68),[69](#bib69) However, the structural portfolio was complemented by AAVrh.39, which differs from AAV10 by only a single aa substitution (A589T) in the VP3 region.[70](#bib70) In addition, the capsid structures of AAVrh.8, AAVrh.10, AAVrh.4282, AAVhu.37, and AAVhu.69 have been determined[70](#bib70),[71](#bib71),[72](#bib72),[73](#bib73),[74](#bib74) ([Table S1](#mmc1)). While the aa sequence identity of primate AAV capsids ranges from 51% to 99%,[38](#bib38) their capsids may appear identical to the untrained eye. However, a distinguishable feature of these capsids is the shape of the 3-fold protrusions. These can protrude radially further outward and appear more “pointed,” as in the case of AAV2, or be radially shorter and more “rounded,” as seen in the AAV5 capsid ([Figure 3](#fig3)A), due to differences in loop conformations, deletions, and/or insertions in the surface loops. This results in AAV2 being one of the largest AAV capsids in terms of its maximum diameter (∼30 nm) and AAV5 one of the smaller AAV capsids (∼28 nm). However, the diameters at the 2-fold depression are essentially the same for both capsids (∼23 nm). These structural differences can be further visualized when the VP monomers are superposed using secondary structure matching algorithms[75](#bib75) ([Figure 3](#fig3)B). While the core of the capsids, with their secondary structure elements, are nearly identical for all primate-based AAVs, significant structural differences are observed in their surface loops. These similarities and differences result in an average root-mean-square deviation of the Cα atom positions (Cα RMSD) of ∼0.8 Å for the VP structures of the 18 primate AAV capsids.\n\n## The variable regions of the AAVs\n\nIn 2006, the determination of the second capsid structure (AAV4) enabled structural comparison between two different AAVs.[66](#bib66) Using secondary structure matching, the VP3 models of AAV2 and AAV4 were superposed, resulting in an overall Cα RMSD of 1.0 Å.[66](#bib66) While the capsid core showed nearly exact overlap of both structures’ coordinates, significant positional differences were observed in the surface loops. Nine regions of structural variability were identified between the superposed capsids, defined as two or more consecutive aa with Cα position displacements greater than 1 Å apart. The nine variable regions (VRs) were originally numbered with Roman numerals; however, some publications also use Arabic numerals.[38](#bib38),[76](#bib76) The locations of the VRs within the VP structures have remained consistent even after the determination of many additional AAV capsid structures ([Figure 3](#fig3)B; [Table S2](#mmc1)) and are as follows: VR-I is located in the BC-loop, VR-II in the DE-loop, VR-III in the EF-loop, VR-IV to -VIII in the large GH-loop, and VR-IX near the C terminus following the βI-strand ([Figure 4](#fig4)). Within the capsid, VR-I, -III, and -XI form the 2/5-fold wall; VR-II forms the channel at the 5-fold symmetry axes; VR-IV, -V, and -VIII interact with each other to form the 3-fold protrusions; and VR-VI and -VII are located at the base of the 3-fold protrusions.\n\nIt should be noted that if the first description of the VRs had been based on a comparison of the AAV2 and AAV5 capsid structures, the HI-loop would have been included among the VRs, whereas VR-VIII would not, as this loop does not meet the definition given above for these AAVs.[57](#bib57) Thus, this review revisits the VRs across the full panel of primate AAVs for which capsid structures have been determined. For this purpose, the VP structures of 18 unique AAV capsids ([Figure 3](#fig3)B) were superposed to identify the first and last aa of each VR where at least one capsid shows a divergence of ≥ 1Å in its Cα aa position within the loop structure. As before, only stretches of more than two consecutive aa with this divergence were considered VRs.[66](#bib66) Due to the larger number of AAV structures now available, the VR aa ranges have expanded from the previous description ([Table S2](#mmc1)). The VRs are the segments of the VP3 sequences that exhibit the highest aa diversity within the capsid when compared across all AAVs and serve as determinants of their cell and tissue tropism as well as differential antigenic properties. However, the aa differences are not uniformly distributed. Some surface loops, such as VR-II, -III, and the HI-loop, are more conserved, as indicated by the larger letter sizes in the frequency plots, while others, such as VR-IV and VR-VIII, are highly variable ([Figure 5](#fig5)). Additionally, variations in loop length among the AAVs contribute to the structural heterogeneity of the VRs, whereas loops of the same length tend to have more similar loop conformations (e.g., VR-II and VR-VIII). The most pronounced structural variations are often observed at the apices of the VRs. However, some VRs, such as VR-IV, VR-V, and VR-IX, do not only have a single apex but are composed of two sub-loops. Among the analyzed capsids, AAV5 has the highest aa divergence from the other AAVs, which often results in unique loop conformations (e.g., VR-I, VR-V, VR-VII, and HI-loop, [Figure 5](#fig5): gray ribbon). Furthermore, clade-specific loop conformations are observed (e.g., for AAV4, AAV11, and AAV12 in VR-I, VR-IV, VR-V, and -VRVI, [Figure 3](#fig3): red, burgundy, and salmon ribbons). Finally, an analysis of the average aa composition for each loop showed that VR-VI has the highest number of charged aa, due to the presence of the prominent H-K-D-D-E/D-E/D-K/R sequence motif (aa526-532) in most AAVs. Particularly, the substitution of these acidic aa negatively affects the transduction efficiency of AAV vectors.[77](#bib77) However, the exact function of this region is unknown. Substitution of basic residues with neutral or acidic aa has been shown to increase capsid stability in some capsids.[78](#bib78),[79](#bib79) In contrast to VR-VI, the loop of VR-III has the fewest charged aa. Regarding the percentage of hydrophobic aa, the VRs are comparable, ranging between ∼30% and 40%. While hydrophobicity in the surface loops is limited due to solvent exposure, the roles of surface-exposed hydrophobic residues in capsid assembly, stability, aggregation, receptor interaction, and transduction efficiency are not fully characterized.\n\n### Figure 5.\n\n[![Figure 5](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/30b057d5d717/gr5.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr5.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig5/)\n\nThe variable regions of the AAVs\n\nRibbon diagrams of the primate AAVs are shown, colored as in [Figure 3](#fig3) for the individual VRs and the HI-loop. The N- and C-terminal ends and the loop apex of each VR are indicated. To the right, amino acid frequency plots are shown for the VRs. Hydrophobic amino acids are colored orange, basic blue, acidic red, polar green, and glycine and proline are colored gray. The percentage of hydrophobic, polar, basic, acidic amino acids, as well as the length of the shortest and longest loops, are provided.\n\n## The capsid structures of nonprimate based AAVs\n\nIn addition to the primates AAVs, several other viruses of the genus *Dependoparvovirus* have been isolated from a wide range of vertebrates, including cattle, goats, pigs, sea lions, foxes, bats, rodents, birds, and reptiles.[64](#bib64),[80](#bib80),[81](#bib81),[82](#bib82),[83](#bib83),[84](#bib84),[85](#bib85),[86](#bib86),[87](#bib87),[88](#bib88) The first two identified nonprimate AAVs were bovine AAV (BAAV) and quail AAV (QAAV, formerly known as avian AAV) in the 1970s, which were found as contaminants of bovine adenovirus type 1 and in preparations of the Olson strain of quail bronchitis, an avian adenovirus, respectively.[89](#bib89),[90](#bib90) The nonprimate AAVs can be as divergent as AAV5 is from the other AAV serotypes. One example is bat AAV isolate 10HB (BtAAV-10HB), with a VP1 sequence identity ranging from 53% to 60% compared to AAV1–AAV13. In 2020, the BtAAV-10HB capsid was the first nonprimate AAV structure determined, at a resolution of 3.0 Å.[91](#bib91) Since then, the capsids of serpentine AAV (SAAV), QAAV, goat AAV isolate 1 (AAVgo.1), goose parvovirus (GPV), and the porcine isolates 1 and 6 (AAVpo.1 and AAVpo.6) have been structurally characterized[92](#bib92),[93](#bib93),[94](#bib94),[95](#bib95),[96](#bib96) ([Table S3](#mmc1)). As for other members of the *Dependoparvovirus* genus, these viruses are nonpathogenic, with the exception of GPV, which is considered the causative agent of Derzy’s disease in waterfowl.[97](#bib97) The VP1 aa sequence identities of the different nonprimate AAVs range between 47% and 94% when compared to AAV2 and AAV5 ([Figure 6](#fig6)A). Overall, the capsid structures of the nonprimate AAVs display the conserved features shared among all AAVs and parvoviruses in general, including a channel at the 5-fold symmetry axis, depressions at the 2-fold symmetry axes, and protrusions around the 3-fold symmetry axes ([Figure 6](#fig6)B). The currently analyzed capsids have, for the most part, more rounded (AAV5-like) 3-fold protrusions, likely due to their shorter VR-IV loops ([Figure 6](#fig6)C). Similar to the primate AAVs, the VP structures show a high level of overlap, with the core of the capsids being nearly identical and structural variability primarily observed in the surface loops.\n\n### Figure 6.\n\n[![Figure 6](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/f34b9830c4d0/gr6.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr6.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig6/)\n\nCapsid structures of the nonprimate AAVs\n\n(A) A cladogram of the nonprimate AAVs is shown, with structures available in the PDB, generated using NGPhylogeny.fr. AAV2 and AAV5 are added for reference (B) Exemplary capsid structures of SAAV (PDB: [7U94](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/7U94)), GPV (PDB: [9ME0](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/9ME0)), AAVpo.6 (PDB: [9NRP](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/9NRP)), and AAVgo.1 (PDB: [7TI4](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/7TI4)) are shown as surface representations generated from their respective PDB files. The capsids are colored radially (blue to red) based on the distance from the center of the capsid. The image was generated in ChimeraX. (C) A structural superposition of nonprimate-based AAVs, colored as indicated, is depicted as ribbon diagrams. The positions of the β-strands, N-termini, C-termini, VRs, and icosahedral 2-fold, 3-fold, and 5-fold symmetry axes are labeled. This figure was generated in PyMOL.\n\nThe nonprimate AAVs are being studied as alternative vectors for gene therapy applications, as pre-existing antibodies in the general human population exclude some patients from enrolling in AAV clinical trials. Currently, all approved AAV-based therapeutics are derived from primate AAVs. It was hypothesized that AAVs that do not disseminate in the human or primate population could circumvent pre-existing human antibodies. However, recent studies with human sera suggest a high level of cross-reactivity, with ∼25%–35% of a panel of human sera recognizing SAAV or QAAV.[92](#bib92),[93](#bib93) In most cases, the sera recognizing these AAVs also cross-reacted with most AAV serotypes, indicating that the highly conserved 5-fold region of the capsids is responsible. Similarly, a panel of human monoclonal antibodies obtained from Zolgensma recipients[98](#bib98) showed that antibodies targeting the 5-fold symmetry axes cross-react with several nonprimate-based AAV capsids.[93](#bib93),[95](#bib95),[96](#bib96) While some of these viruses are naturally able to transduce human cells or tissues[99](#bib99) others are unable to do so (e.g., QAAV). However, engineered QAAV capsids in which the VP1u region was swapped with that of AAV8 restored transduction in human cells.[100](#bib100) This strategy also worked with the capsids of SAAV and bearded dragon parvovirus (BDPV).[100](#bib100) Overall, the nonprimate AAVs are understudied, and only a small fraction of dependoparvoviruses has been characterized for their potential as future gene therapy vectors.\n\n## Mapping the capsid’s interactions\n\nThe ability of AAV vectors to deliver their payload to target cells is determined by a series of interactions between the capsid and components of the immune system, cell attachment factors or surface receptors, as well as intracellular trafficking proteins that enable the transport of the transgene to the nucleus. These interactions are being characterized to obtain insights into the capsids’ functions and to improve the efficiency and specificity of AAV vectors. One of the most significant hurdles to the broad utilization of AAV vectors is the presence of pre-existing neutralizing antibodies (NAbs) in a large percentage of healthy individuals. These antibodies originate from prior infection with naturally circulating AAVs. Antibodies targeting the capsid can inactivate the vectors and lead to a loss of treatment efficacy. Thus, patients with NAbs and/or those exceeding defined antibody titer thresholds are excluded from receiving AAV therapeutics.[101](#bib101),[102](#bib102) A strategy to circumvent these pre-existing NAbs is the engineering of AAV capsids capable of evading this immune response. Prior to the “resolution revolution” in cryo-EM, the binding sites of mouse monoclonal antibodies were mapped to the capsids of AAV1, AAV2, AAV4, AAV5, AAV6, AAV8, AAV9, and AAVrh.10 at resolutions ranging from ∼5 to 20 Å.[103](#bib103),[104](#bib104),[105](#bib105),[106](#bib106),[107](#bib107),[108](#bib108),[109](#bib109),[110](#bib110),[111](#bib111),[112](#bib112) At these resolutions, models of previously determined high-resolution capsid and antibody structures were fitted into the lower-resolution maps to identify a shortlist of potential aa in the capsid epitope for the analyzed antibody. The improvement of the cryo-EM technology enabled the direct identification of the contact aa on the capsid surface that interact with the complementarity-determining regions (CDRs) of the fragment antigen-binding (Fab) domains of antibodies at high resolution. The first high-resolution AAV-antibody complex structure was that of AAV5 with the mouse monoclonal antibody HL2476, which binds to the capsid’s 3-fold protrusions[113](#bib113) ([Table S4](#mmc1)). More recently, the complex structures of a panel of 21 human monoclonal antibodies, derived from three patients who received the biologic Zolgensma, bound to the AAV9 capsid were determined.[114](#bib114) Among these 21 complex structures, the vast majority (>75%) of the antibodies bound near the 2-fold region of the capsid, with two antibodies binding to the 3- and 5-fold regions each, and another one to the 2/5-fold wall (Fab1-2, [Figure 7](#fig7)A). Another observation from these structures was that ligands binding at or close to the symmetry axes of the capsid cannot be resolved by applying global icosahedral symmetry during 3D image reconstruction. Instead, localized reconstruction of sub-particles for the region of interest is required to resolve the ligands without “blurring.” The interface area for these antibodies ranged between ∼1000 and 2000 Å2 (average: ∼1500 Å2) ([Figure 7](#fig7)A).\n\n### Figure 7.\n\n[![Figure 7](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/f463/12682079/c96df17fe17e/gr7.jpg)](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&p=PMC3&id=12682079_gr7.jpg)\n\n[Open in a new tab](figure/fig7/)\n\nAAV capsid complex structures\n\nExemplary complex capsid structures of (A) AAV9 with Fab 1–2 (PDB: [9B6O](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/9B6O)), (B) AAV8 with AAVX (PDB: [9DC3](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/9DC3)), (C) AAV2 with AAVR (PDB: [6NZ0](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/6NZ0)), and (D) AAV1 with SIA (sialic acid) (PDB: [5EGC](https://www.rcsb.org/structure/https://www.rcsb.org/structure/5EGC)) are shown as surface representations generated from their respective PDB files. The capsids are colored radially (blue to red) based on the distance from the center of the capsid, and the ligands are colored purple, magenta, light purple, and light pink, respectively. Below each capsid complex, the binding site is zoomed in, with the surfaces of the ligands rendered transparent. SIA is depicted as stick atoms, while the other ligands are shown as black ribbon diagrams without secondary structure elements. Under each complex, the calculated interface areas (in Å2) are shown.\n\nSimilar to the Fabs of human or murine antibodies, the binding of single-chain camelid VH domain antibodies, also referred to as nanobodies,[115](#bib115) has been mapped to AAV capsids. These nanobodies are only about a quarter of the size of the Fab fragment of a standard IgG and are used in affinity chromatography purification protocols, enabling scalable \"Good Manufacturing Practice\" purification of AAV vectors. The most commonly utilized nanobodies, AVB and AAVX, enable the purification of a wide range of AAV capsids.[116](#bib116),[117](#bib117) Their binding sites have been mapped at high resolution to the AAV8 capsid (AVB: 2.7 Å, AAVX: 2.3 Å) ([Figure 7](#fig7)B; [Table S4](#mmc1)) and at lower resolution (4–9 Å) to the capsids of AAV1 (AVB and AAVX), AAV2, and AAV5 (only AVB).[118](#bib118),[119](#bib119) Both bind to the 5-fold region of the capsids and are structurally nearly identical but differ in their ability to purify the capsids of AAV9.[116](#bib116),[117](#bib117) Additionally, nanobodies specifically for the purification of AAV8 and AAV9 have been developed. Cryo-EM structures of these ligands bound to their respective capsids at ∼3–4 Å resolution show them interacting with the 2/5-fold wall (Capture Select AAV8) or the 5-fold region (Capture Select AAV9).[118](#bib118) Due to their smaller size, the interface area of the nanobodies with the AAV capsids is usually smaller than that of human or murine IgGs ([Figure 7](#fig7)B). These complex structures identified the critical capsid aa required for purification of an AAV sample and will guide future capsid engineering efforts to ensure compatibility of new AAV vectors with affinity purification resins.\n\nAs with antibodies, several molecules such as glycan and proteinaceous receptors have been complexed with AAV capsids to further understand the vectors’ cell and tissue tropism. The binding sites of several protein receptors have been determined structurally. One membrane protein that is important for the transduction of many AAVs is KIAA0319L, also known as AAVR.[120](#bib120) Its extracellular portion contains five Ig-like polycystic kidney disease (PKD) domains. Constructs comprising PKD1-5 or PKD1-2 have been complexed with the capsids of AAV1, -2, -5, -9, AAVgo.1, and the engineered capsid PHP.eB (see [the capsid structures of engineered AAVs](#sec7) section), and cryo-EM structures have been determined at resolutions ranging from 2.4 to 3.8 Å.[94](#bib94),[121](#bib121),[122](#bib122),[123](#bib123),[124](#bib124),[125](#bib125),[126](#bib126) For AAV1, -2, -9, and PHP.eB, the PKD2 domain of AAVR interacted with the capsids in the same binding mode, along the side of the 3-fold protrusions and on top of the 2/5-fold wall ([Figure 7](#fig7)C), whereas AAV5 and AAVgo.1 both bound to the PKD1 domain around the 5-fold axis of the capsids toward the 2/5-fold wall. Another membrane protein complexed with an AAV capsid is carbonic anhydrase 4 (Car4). This protein is anchored to the cell surface via a glycosylphosphatidylinositol linkage,[127](#bib127) which was identified as a potential receptor for the engineered capsids AAV9-P31 (see [the capsid structures of engineered AAVs](#sec7) section) and AAV9-P36.[128](#bib128) Recombinant Car4 was found to bind to the 3-fold protrusions of AAV9-P31. Due to its size, similar to antibodies bound close to the symmetry axis, a localized cryo-EM reconstruction approach was needed to resolve the complex at 1.8 Å resolution.[129](#bib129) Recently, the transmembrane protein carboxypeptidase D, also referred to as AAVR2, was identified as an alternative receptor for AAV8.[130](#bib130) The binding site of AAVR2 was mapped to the AAV8 capsid in the same study, which showed it interacting with the 3-fold protrusions across the 2-fold symmetry axes at a resolution of 2.9 Å.\n\nCurrently, all high-resolution cryo-EM structures for capsid complexes are determined using single-particle reconstructions, which involve the structural averaging of a large number of homogeneous particles. However, the flexible nature of some proteins interacting with the AAV capsid using this technique results in them being averaged out and thus not detectable. Some studies have turned to cryo-electron tomography (cryo-ET) to reveal these dynamic interactions, but technical limitations restrict the achievable resolution.[131](#bib131) The first application of cryo-ET analyzed the binding of the PKD1-PKD2 domain of AAVR to the AAV2 and AAV5 capsids.[126](#bib126) The resulting ∼20 Å structures revealed four potential configurations of the PKD1 domain in the case of the AAV2 capsid and the tightly bound PKD2 domain. However, the flexible PKD1 configurations are all different from those seen in AAV5. For the AAV5-PKD1-PKD2 complex, PKD1 binds tightly, but the sub-tomograms showed three conformations of the previously missing PKD2 domain extending away from the surface of the capsid. In another study, cryo-ET was used to determine the binding site of human interleukin 3 (IL3) at the side of the 3-fold protrusion adjacent to the 2-fold depression on the AAV9 capsid.[132](#bib132) IL3 is an immunomodulatory protein produced by activated T cells, and thus this interaction has implications for the host immune response and tolerance when receiving AAV-based gene therapy treatments.\n\nLastly, the binding modes of AAV6 and AAVpo.1 to importin alpha (IMPα) were determined using X-ray crystallography.[133](#bib133),[134](#bib134) This interaction is mediated by the structurally disordered region within the VP1u and VP1/2 common region; thus, only a short segment (aa115–174) was utilized for the complex instead of the full capsid. In this structure, IMPα interacted with the nuclear localization signals (BR1 and BR3) of the tested AAVs. At the end of the intracellular trafficking pathway, IMPα is responsible for the import of AAV capsids into the nucleus, and understanding this interaction may provide opportunities for developing engineered AAV vectors with improved nuclear import characteristics.\n\nMany of the AAVs have been shown to utilize glycans for cell surface attachment.[107](#bib107),[135](#bib135),[136](#bib136),[137](#bib137),[138](#bib138),[139](#bib139),[140](#bib140),[141](#bib141) While some of their binding sites were characterized by mutational analysis, others were identified structurally. For sialic acid (SIA), the binding sites on the AAV1 and AAV5 capsids were determined by X-ray crystallography[142](#bib142),[143](#bib143) and for SAAV by cryo-EM.[92](#bib92) In each of these complex maps, SIA bound differently to the capsids: at the base of the 3-fold protrusions in the case of AAV1 ([Figure 7](#fig7)D), in proximity to the 3-fold axis for AAV5, and on the 2/5-fold wall in the SAAV capsid. Heparin or related analogs have been mapped to the capsids of AAV2 and AAV3 at low resolution, showing their binding at or near the capsids’ 3-fold-protrusion.[144](#bib144),[145](#bib145),[146](#bib146) Similarly, a synthetic heparinoid was complexed with the engineered AAV-DJ capsid (see [the capsid structures of engineered AAVs](#sec7) section) by cryo-EM at 2.8 Å resolution, showing density for the glycan at the apices of the 3-fold protrusions.[147](#bib147) For the AAV9 capsid complexed with a terminal galactose glycan and the AAVrh.10 capsid with a sulfated LacNAc tetrasaccharide, cryo-EM structures at resolutions ranging from 2.4–3.1 Å showed density at the base of the 3-fold protrusions,[107](#bib107),[148](#bib148) the same pocket where AAV1 and AAV6 bind to SIA molecules ([Figure 7](#fig7)D).[142](#bib142) Overall, the AAV capsids predominantly interact with glycans and proteinaceous receptors at or near their 3-fold protrusions and the 2/5-fold wall, which generally display greater aa and structural diversity among the AAVs, resulting in varying host, tissue, and cell tropism. The identification of these detailed receptor interactions enables the engineering of AAV capsids to either de-target the vector from a particular receptor, or conversely, enhance the interaction for improved transduction efficiency.\n\n## The capsid structures of engineered AAVs\n\n\n...198052 bytes truncated...\n\nThe tool call succeeded but the output was truncated. Full output saved to: /home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_15c2a631d001HoR624FHoqq6ZH\nUse Grep to search the full content or Read with offset/limit to view specific sections.","metadata":{"truncated":true,"outputPath":"/home/hal/.local/share/opencode/tool-output/tool_15c2a631d001HoR624FHoqq6ZH"},"title":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12682079 (text/html; charset=utf-8)","time":{"start":1792547643455,"end":1792547644193}},"id":"prt_15c2a5dfd00156jIH4xOFDt4Fz","sessionID":"ses_ea3e01b08ffe25JrGjIjfiH29d","messageID":"msg_15c1fe97f001ItZDCL4jdgkjsd"}}
